[[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]] was assassinated in the theatre at Aigai, and it was there that his son [[ Alexander the Great ]] was proclaimed king of [[ Macedon ]]. !!Aegae!! or !!Aigai!! (Ancient Greek: Αἰγαί, //Aigaí//, [[ Latinized | latinisation-of-names ]]: //Aegae//, Greek: Αιγές, [[ romanized | romanization-of-greek ]]: //Aiges//) was the original capital of that kingdom, the [[ ancient Greek | ancient-greece ]] realm of the [[ Argead | argead-dynasty ]] [[ Makedones ]] in [[ Lower Macedonia ]]. The city sat in the [[ Haliacmon ]] valley on the foothills of the [[ Pierian Mountains ]], between the modern towns of [[ Vergina ]] and Palatitsia,[[ ^1 ]][[ ^2 ]] looking out over the central [[ Macedonian | macedonia-greece ]] plain in northern [[ Greece ]].

The city was abandoned in the 3rd century AD and found again in the 19th.[[ ^1 ]][[ ^3 ]] Three major archaeological missions have dug there since. [[ Léon Heuzey ]] of the [[ French School at Athens ]] led the first in the 1860s. Over a century later [[ Manolis Andronikos ]] led excavations that made many important discoveries, among them the tomb of Philip II of Macedon and the [[ Golden Larnax ]] bearing the [[ Vergina Sun ]]. Angeliki Kottaridi led restoration efforts in the 2000s. Today it is an [[ archaeological site ]] with two museums, and in 1996 it was inscribed on the [[ UNESCO ]] [[ World Heritage List ]] for its monumental significance in [[ Western civilization ]] and its exceptional architecture.[[ ^3 ]]

== Name

The name Aigai is etymologically related to the word for 'goat' (Ancient Greek: [[ αἴξ | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/αἴξ#Ancient_Greek ]], [[ romanized | romanization-of-ancient-greek ]]: //aíx//), and is translated as 'goat town'.[[ ^4 ]] [[ Diodorus Siculus ]] claimed the city got its name after [[ Perdiccas I | perdiccas-i-of-macedon ]] was instructed by the [[ Pythia ]] at the [[ Oracle of Delphi ]] to found his city at a place where "white-horned goats rest at dawn".[[ ^5 ]][[ ^6 ]] This version, supported by [[ Thucydides ]], was the 'official' [[ founding myth ]] in the 5th century BC.[[ ^4 ]] [[ Justin | justin-historian ]], in the //[[ Epitome of the Philippic History | epitome-of-the-philippic-history-of-pompeius-trogus ]]//, tells it differently: [[ Edessa | edessa-greece ]] was the older name of Aegae before [[ Caranus of Macedon ]] captured it.[[ ^7 ]] Because of that account, Edessa was historically thought to have been the site of Aegae.[[ ^8 ]]

The city also turns up in the historical record as !!Aegeae/Aigeai!! (Αἰγέαι) and !!Aegaea/Aigaia!! (Αἰγαῖα).[[ ^9 ]] The name is plural, as ancient Greek place names often are: [[ Athens ]] ([[ Ἀθῆναι | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Ἀθῆναι#Ancient_Greek ]], //Athenai//) and [[ Thebes | thebes-greece ]] ([[ Θῆβαι | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Θῆβαι#Ancient_Greek ]], //Thebai//) are the same. In modern Greek it is !!Aiges!! (Αιγές, Greek pronunciation: [[ [eˈʝes\] | https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Help%3AIPA%2FGreek ]]), the ancient plural ending //-ai// having become the modern //-es// in a typical [[ first declension | first-declension#greek-nouns ]] transition.

== History

In the 7th century BC the [[ Temenids | temenus ]]' dominance led the Macedonians to expand and subdue the local populations, a process that ran until the end of the 6th century BC and established the dynasty at Aigai. Where the [[ Argead dynasty ]] came from is something the ancient sources disagree about.[[ ^10 ]][[ ^11 ]] [[ Alexander I | alexander-i-of-macedon ]] is the first truly historic figure; counting back along the line of succession, the beginnings of the Macedonian dynasty have traditionally been dated to 750 BC.[[ ^12 ]] [[ Herodotus ]] says[[ ^13 ]] the [[ Argead dynasty ]] was an ancient Greek royal house led by [[ Perdiccas I ]], who fled from [[ Argos | ancient-argos ]] in about 650 BC.[[ ^14 ]]

The Macedonians saw the state the Argeads founded as a typical ancient Greek city-state (//[[ Polis ]]//): Aegae the urban centre ([[ ἄστυ | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ἄστυ#Ancient_Greek ]]) ruling over the surrounding countryside ([[ χώρα | https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/χώρα#Ancient_Greek ]]), not basically different from [[ Athens | classical-athens ]] or [[ Sparta ]]. How far that perception was historically accurate is not clear.[[ ^15 ]] What the archaeology shows is a place that developed as an organized collection of villages, laid out to mirror the aristocratic structure of tribes centred on the power of the king, and it stayed that way throughout its history. Aigai never became a large city. Most of its inhabitants lived in the surrounding villages, and the walled //asty// (acropolis) was built at the centre. From Aigai the Macedonians spread into the central part of Macedonia and displaced the local population of [[ Pierians ]]. From 513 to 480 BC Aigai was part of the [[ Persian Empire ]], but [[ Amyntas I ]] managed to keep its relative independence, avoid [[ satrapy | satrap ]] and extend its possessions. In the first half of the 5th century BC Aigai was the capital of Macedon. Life reached unseen levels of luxury, and to meet the needs of the court merchants from all over the ancient world brought valuable goods to Aigai: perfume, carved ornaments and jewellery.[[ ^16 ]] The city wall went up in the 5th century, probably under [[ Perdiccas II ]]. At the end of that century [[ Archelaus I | archelaus-i-of-macedon ]] brought artists, poets and philosophers from all over the Greek world to his court; it was at Aigai that [[ Euripides ]] wrote and presented his last tragedies.

At the beginning of the 4th century BC, Archelaus moved the Macedonian capital northeast to [[ Pella ]], on a coastal waterway of the [[ Thermaic Gulf ]] on the [[ central Macedonian | central-macedonia ]] plain.[[ ^17 ]] Aigai kept its role as the sacred city of the Macedonian kingdom all the same: the site of the traditional cult centres, a royal palace and the royal tombs. That is why [[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]] was there, attending the wedding of his daughter [[ Cleopatra | cleopatra-of-macedon ]] to [[ King Alexander of Epirus | alexander-i-of-epirus ]], when he fell by the hand of [[ Pausanias of Orestis ]][[ ^18 ]] in the theatre.[[ ^19 ]] His son [[ Alexander the Great ]] was proclaimed king of Macedonia on the spot. The old capital had remained the "national hearth"[[ ^20 ]] of the Macedonian kingdom and the burial place of its kings, who descended from [[ Perdiccas | perdiccas-i-of-macedon ]]. Alexander's own body was to have rested at Aigai too,[[ ^21 ]] but [[ Ptolemy I Soter ]] took it to [[ Memphis | memphis-egypt ]]. The urban fabric of the city represents a pivotal moment of transition between the classical Greek city-state and the imperial centres of the [[ Hellenistic period ]]. The [[ bitter struggles | wars-of-the-diadochi ]] between the [[ heirs of Alexander | diadochi ]] in the 3rd century adversely affected the city; in 276 BC [[ Gallic | gauls ]] mercenaries of [[ Pyrrhus | pyrrhus-of-epirus ]] plundered many of the tombs.

When the Romans overthrew the Macedonian kingdom [[ in 168 BC | battle-of-pydna ]], both the old and the new capital were destroyed, the walls pulled down and all the buildings burned. In the 1st century AD a landslide destroyed what had been rebuilt (excavations establish that parts were still inhabited then). Between the 2nd and 5th centuries AD the population gradually moved from the foothills of the [[ Pierian range | pierian-mountains ]] down to the plain, until all that remained was a small settlement whose name alone, Palatitsia (palace), indicated its former importance.

== Tomb of Philip II and other Royal Tombs of Aigai

Before the discoveries at [[ Vergina ]], [[ Edessa | edessa-greece ]] was thought to be the site of Aigai. In 1977 the Greek archaeologist [[ Manolis Andronikos ]] began excavating the Great Tumulus at Aigai[[ ^22 ]] and found that two of the four tombs inside it had lain undisturbed since antiquity. Both of them, and Tomb II in particular, held fabulous treasures and objects of great quality and sophistication.[[ ^23 ]]

There was much debate for some years,[[ ^24 ]][[ ^25 ]] but Tomb II has been shown to be that of Philip II, as indicated by many features,[[ ^26 ]] among them the [[ greaves | greave ]]: one of them was shaped to fit a leg with a misaligned tibia, and Philip II was recorded as having broken his tibia. The remains of the skull, too, show damage to the right eye caused by the penetration of an object, historically recorded to be an arrow.[[ ^27 ]]

The most recent research gives further evidence that Tomb II contains the remains of [[ Philip II | philip-ii-of-macedon ]].[[ ^28 ]]

== Palace of Aigai

The recently excavated palace is considered to be not only the biggest but, together with the [[ Parthenon ]], one of the most significant buildings of [[ classical Greece ]].[[ ^29 ]] It is the most important building discovered at the site. It stands on a plateau directly below the acropolis, a building of two or perhaps three stories centred on a large open courtyard flanked by Doric colonnades. On the north side a large gallery looked out over the stage of the neighbouring theatre and the whole Macedonian plain. The decoration was sumptuous: mosaic floors, painted plastered walls and fine relief tiles, with the masonry and architectural members covered in high-quality marble stucco. Excavations have dated its construction to the reign of Philip II,[[ ^30 ]] even though he also had a palace in the capital, [[ Pella ]]. It has been suggested that the building was designed by the architect [[ Pytheos of Priene | pythius-of-priene ]], known for his work on the [[ Mausoleum at Halicarnassus ]] and for his views on urban planning and architectural proportions. The theatre, also from the second half of the 4th century BC, was closely associated with the palace.

Nearly 30 large columns that surrounded the palace's main [[ peristyle ]] have been reconstructed, some towering to a height of 25 ft.[[ ^31 ]] The frieze on the peristyle's southern section has been reconstructed as well.[[ ^32 ]] Over 5,000 square feet of [[ mosaics | mosaic ]], depicting a range of scenes including the ravishing of [[ Europa | europa-consort-of-zeus ]] and motifs from nature, have been carefully conserved.

The Palace of Aigai is the largest building of classical Greece, and it is where [[ Alexander the Great ]] was proclaimed king in 336 BC.[[ ^33 ]]

The palace lost its significance for Macedonian royalty after it, and the rest of the city, was burned down in 168 BCE after the [[ Battle of Pydna ]], even though the city itself remained for another three centuries.[[ ^34 ]] A landslide in the first century AD preserved what was left of the ruins, leaving about 3–4 m of soil on top of the monument.[[ ^34 ]]

The Palace of Aigai reopened to the public in January 2024 after an extensive 16-year restoration project.[[ ^33 ]]

